            "<strong>Breast awareness</strong> — a woman knowing what her breasts normally look and feel like at different times of her cycle and reporting any change promptly — has replaced the rigid monthly ‘self-examination’ ritual, but the technique is still taught: looking in a mirror with arms at the sides, raised, and hands pressed on the hips for a change in size or shape, skin dimpling or puckering, redness, an orange-peel texture, a nipple newly pulled in or pointing differently, or a rash on the nipple; then feeling each breast lying down with the flat pads of the three middle fingers in small circles at light, medium and deep pressure over the whole breast from collarbone to bra line and from breastbone to armpit, including the tail into the axilla, and gently squeezing the nipple for discharge. The <strong>clinical breast examination</strong> by the nurse follows the same sequence with the woman sitting and then supine with her arm behind her head, adds the palpation of the axillary and supraclavicular nodes, and records any lump by position on the clock face and distance from the nipple, size, consistency, mobility, tenderness and skin attachment; it is offered yearly to women over 25 at any clinic contact, during antenatal and postnatal care, and in the October awareness campaigns run by Breast Care International, the Ghana Health Service and many churches, and where mammography is available it is offered every one to two years from 40 to 50 onwards. Most lumps are benign. The <strong>fibroadenoma</strong> is the smooth, firm, rubbery, very mobile ‘breast mouse’ of the woman under 30, which may be left alone once confirmed or removed if large or growing; <strong>breast cysts</strong> are smooth, sometimes tender fluctuant lumps of the woman in her forties that aspiration empties and diagnoses; <strong>fibrocystic change</strong> is the lumpy, tender, cyclical breast that needs reassurance, a supportive bra and simple analgesia; <strong>mastitis</strong> in the breastfeeding woman is a hot, red, painful wedge with fever treated by continuing to feed or express from that breast, flucloxacillin for 10 to 14 days, analgesia and rest, and a <strong>breast abscess</strong> — a fluctuant tender mass with fever — by aspiration under ultrasound or incision and drainage with the milk still expressed; <strong>duct ectasia and papilloma</strong> cause nipple discharge, and <strong>fat necrosis</strong> after trauma produces a hard irregular lump that mimics cancer. The rule that protects the woman is <strong>triple assessment</strong> of every discrete lump — clinical examination, imaging (ultrasound under 35, mammography and ultrasound over 35), and tissue by fine-needle aspiration cytology or <strong>core needle biopsy</strong> — and a lump is not called benign on examination alone, whatever the woman's age. A blood-stained or single-duct nipple discharge, a nipple eczema that does not clear (Paget's disease), skin dimpling, an axillary lump, and a hot swollen breast in a woman who is not breastfeeding (inflammatory cancer) are referred as urgently as a lump.",
